/* ------------------------------------------------------------
   4. BORDERS
   Region/territory outlines. Points use the same x/y percentage
   system as MARKERS, so you can gather them with the coordinate
   picker just like marker positions.

   - closed: true  -> a filled region (polygon), e.g. a kingdom's territory
   - closed: false -> an open line (polyline), e.g. a road or river
   - dashArray: e.g. "6,4" for a dashed line, or null for solid

   EXCLAVES / MULTI-PART SHAPES: `points` can also be an array of
   separate point-arrays instead of one flat list, letting a single
   named region be made of disconnected pieces that still share one
   color/popup/hover behavior. Example:

     points: [
       [ { x: 15, y: 30 }, { x: 42, y: 22 }, { x: 40, y: 60 } ],  // main body
       [ { x: 70, y: 10 }, { x: 78, y: 10 }, { x: 78, y: 18 } ]   // exclave
     ]

   NESTED REGIONS (country > state > county, etc.): add a `parent`
   field with another border's `id`. Parents automatically render
   underneath their children (so children stay clickable), and a
   child's popup shows the full breadcrumb, e.g. "Eastern Realm ›
   Callow Province". There's no limit to nesting depth.

   AUTO-GENERATED PARENT BORDERS: instead of manually tracing a
   country's outline around a dozen states, set `autoGenerate: true`
   on the parent and give it NO `points` at all. At render time it's
   computed as the geometric union of every border whose `parent`
   points at it -- so as you add/edit/remove state borders, the
   country outline updates itself automatically. Example:

     { id: "usa", name: "United States", autoGenerate: true,
       color: "#2a6ebb", closed: true, weight: 2, dashArray: null,
       fillOpacity: 0.05 },
     { id: "ca", name: "California", parent: "usa", points: [...] },
     { id: "nv", name: "Nevada",     parent: "usa", points: [...] }

   COMPACT SHAPES: instead of `points: [ { x: .., y: .. }, ... ]` (about
   24 characters per vertex) an entry may give `shape: "..."`, the short
   string the Border Generator writes (about 2 characters per vertex, so
   a whole map fits in under 100 KB). Entries that use `shape` also leave
   out what the map can infer:
     - closed is true
     - color is taken from the nearest ancestor that has one
     - weight and fillOpacity come from BORDER_DEFAULTS (below)
   A child therefore looks like:
     { id: "region01-03", name: "Eastern March", parent: "region01", shape: "s5Ek..." }
   Edit mode copies such entries back in the same compact form. Names,
   descriptions and links are edited as usual; don't edit the shape text
   by hand (use edit mode).
   ------------------------------------------------------------ */
